    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d421">

                <pb facs="0516" n="414" sameAs="frus:frus1927v03/pg414"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.51/1845a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Munro</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-10-22T17:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 22,
                                1927—5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>157. It is Cumberland’s<anchor n="26" xml:id="d421-f1"/> plan to resign and
                    undertake financial work in New York, leaving Haiti by December 15. He has
                    informed the Department that he could make a financial survey of Nicaragua in
                    the interim between leaving Haiti and beginning work in New York. It is
                    estimated that the time required for this work is about three months. Both McCoy
                    and Dodds<anchor n="27" xml:id="d421-f2"/> believe this advisable. Department
                    desires your views. Cable.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d421-f1" n="26" xml:id="d421-f1-content">
                <p>Dr. W. W. Cumberland, since 1923 Financial Adviser and Customs Receiver for the
                    Haitian Government.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d421-f2" n="27" xml:id="d421-f2-content">
                <p>Dr. Harold W. Dodds, who accompanied General McCoy on his trip to Nicaragua. Dr.
                    Dodds had been engaged in 1921 by the Nicaraguan Government to assist in the
                    revision of the electoral laws of Nicaragua. See <ref
                        target="frus1923v02#pg_605"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1923, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, pp. 605</ref> ff.; also <ref
                        target="frus1924v02#pg_487"><hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, 1924, vol.
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, pp. 487</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
